        ":question/id": "66ea80ee-e83a-580d-84c5-654898396cd8",
        ":question/problem": "The vectors are drawn on a square grid: $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![Vector diagram](images/d8/d8d5a545d88a83236994219393eb998b67abfa5b05e6f7d0f9c26529f168f2b2.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mu\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$\\mu=$ {{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "![Completed vector route](images/70/7052d3c533e32a07ba1e612d92b7b81b74e6aaf34a12f6ca53a7ce0f313ef787.png)\n\nConstruct the route $O\\to X\\to C$, first parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\nAlong $OX$, move $1$ square left and $1$ square down. A copy of $\\mathbf{a}$ moves $1$ square left and $1$ square down. Thus $OX=1|\\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{OX}=\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nAlong $XC$, move $6$ squares right and $3$ squares up. A copy of $\\mathbf{b}$ moves $4$ squares left and $2$ squares down. Thus $XC=\\frac{3}{2}|\\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\\overrightarrow{XC}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{XC}\\\\&=\\mathbf{a}-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=1$ and $\\mu=-\\frac{3}{2}$."
